<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         THE DIALOG GRAMMAR, ON THE FIRE LAYER. Every dialog in Views/Dialogs
         shares it, exactly the way every settings tab shares
         Views/Settings/SettingsStyles.axaml — and it is deliberately the SAME
         vocabulary one level down, so a dialog opening over Settings or Play
         Mode reads as the same product rather than as a leftover from an
         older build.

         Four levels of hierarchy, and nothing else:

            scrim   → Grid.DialogScrim: the modal backdrop. IsVisible + ZIndex
                      stay inline (each dialog owns its own stacking order).
            card    → Border.DialogCard, wearing Border.FireScreen's shape:
                      radius 18, one hairline, one very deep soft shadow. It
                      holds a Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto".
            head    → Grid.DialogHead: accent icon badge + a CHAKRA PETCH
                      title (+ Space Grotesk subtitle + a mono context line),
                      LEFT aligned. Same 30 px badge as a settings group, on
                      purpose.
            body    → Grid.DlgRow rows: a Space Grotesk 13.5/Bold label (+ an
                      11.5 px description) on the left and exactly ONE control
                      in a fixed-width right column, so every control in a
                      dialog shares one right edge. Rows may be clustered in a
                      Border.DlgSection under a MONO EYEBROW.
            foot    → Border.DialogFooter: a hairline rule, then a left slot
                      for the tertiary action (Back / Reset) and a right slot
                      with the secondary actions and exactly ONE Button.Primary,
                      always last — which is the CHAMFERED Fire primary.

         WHY IT IS ONE FILE: the 38 dialogs each invented their own title size
         (Display / Heading / Title), their own footer margin (10 / 12 / 16 /
         20 / 24 px), their own button MinWidth (80 / 90 / 100 / 120 / 150) and
         their own label-column width (96 / 110 / 120 / 130). Four of them
         re-declared a local `TextBox`/`TextBlock.Label` style verbatim.

         WHY THE FIRE TOKENS AND NOT THE APP'S: the palette and the typeface
         are already global (App.axaml's base Colors are the Fire palette;
         Typography.axaml makes Space Grotesk the inherited default), so what
         was left of the old grammar was its SHAPES — radius 6/14 against the
         layer's 8/10/12/18, a 16 px title against Chakra 20, an app-scale
         12/16/20 rhythm against the Fire 10/12/14/18/22 one. Everything below
         reaches for FireRadius*/FireSize*/FirePad*/FireGap*/FireSpace* ONLY;
         mixing two ladders is how a surface ends up half-converted
         (docs/agents/ui/fire-design-system.md, "the ladders are
         self-contained").

         THE THREE FIRE COMPONENTS THIS FILE REUSES rather than restates —
         because a Fire mark re-invented inline in one file is how two surfaces
         stop being one product:
           • `FirePrimaryButton`   the footer's primary action (the 45° chamfer)
           • `FireToggleSwitch`    every boolean in a row
           • `FireNavRowToggle`    (SettingsShellView's rail; not used here)

         TWO SETTERS THAT MAY NEVER APPEAR HERE, both learned the hard way in
         SettingsStyles.axaml and both applying to any shared style file:
           • no `Cursor` setter — building a Cursor resolves ICursorFactory
             from the platform, which a platform-free test host does not have.
             Set Cursor="Hand" INLINE on the control instead. (The Fire button
             CLASSES do set one; that is fine, they live in the app-scoped
             Styles/Fire.axaml, which is only ever built under a real App.)
           • no `Transitions` setter — AvaloniaList<ITransition>.Add calls
             Dispatcher.VerifyAccess, so the file could only be constructed on
             the UI thread. It is also wrong on principle: dialogs are
             IsVisible-gated surfaces and a hidden control's style clocks keep
             ticking (DotCharter.UI/AGENTS.md).

         WHERE IT IS INCLUDED: ONCE, in Views/Shell/DialogContainerView.axaml —
         every overlay dialog is a child of that host, so one instantiation
         styles all 34 of them (a per-view StyleInclude would re-instantiate
         this set 34 times; see the comment in DialogContainerView about the old
         Theme.axaml double-include). ONE dialog is hosted somewhere else and
         carries its own include: DiagnosticsWindow (a separate top-level
         Window, non-modal on purpose). The count is linted in docs/agents/ui/dialogs.md's terms by
         the includer sweep over Views/Dialogs + Views/Diagnostics.
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <Styles.Resources>
        <!-- State tints. Brush-level Opacity over an EXISTING colour token
             (the PedalStatus*/PanelGlass pattern) — no new `Color` keys, so
             the theme-completeness gate is untouched and every one of these
             re-tints correctly when the user switches theme. The alphas are
             the Fire spec's 8-14 % chip band. -->
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DlgNoticeFill" Color="{DynamicResource WarningColorValue}" Opacity="0.12" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DlgNoticeStroke" Color="{DynamicResource WarningColorValue}" Opacity="0.55" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DlgErrorFill" Color="{DynamicResource DangerColorValue}" Opacity="0.12" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DlgErrorStroke" Color="{DynamicResource DangerColorValue}" Opacity="0.55" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DlgOkFill" Color="{DynamicResource SuccessColorValue}" Opacity="0.12" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DlgOkStroke" Color="{DynamicResource SuccessColorValue}" Opacity="0.55" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DlgInfoFill" Color="{DynamicResource InfoColorValue}" Opacity="0.12" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DlgInfoStroke" Color="{DynamicResource InfoColorValue}" Opacity="0.55" />
    </Styles.Resources>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         THE GRAMMAR ITSELF, BY LEVEL. Every `<Style>` this file used to
         declare inline now lives in `Views/Dialogs/DialogStyles/`, sliced
         along the numbered sections it already carried and moved byte for
         byte: shell + head, body + the honest states, foot.

         ORDER IS THE CONTRACT. Avalonia applies styles in the order it walks
         this collection and a later selector wins, so these includes stand in
         the exact order the sections stood in the one file. The footer's
         `:not(.Primary)` ghost rules are the case that shows why it matters:
         they are written to lose to the primary's ControlTheme on purpose,
         and a shuffle changes which of two rules over one Button wins.

         The `Styles.Resources` block above stays here. It is the eight state
         tints, and `Perf/DialogConstructionUiPerfSmokeTests` asserts every
         one of them resolves from a real dialog inside a real
         `DialogContainerView` — the single place this grammar is included.
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <StyleInclude Source="avares://DotCharter/Views/Dialogs/DialogStyles/ShellAndHead.axaml" />
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://DotCharter/Views/Dialogs/DialogStyles/BodyAndStates.axaml" />
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://DotCharter/Views/Dialogs/DialogStyles/Foot.axaml" />

</Styles>
